# Release signing credentials for the Android build.
#
# Copy this file to `key.properties` (same directory) and fill in the four
# values below. `key.properties` is git-ignored — never commit it, and never
# commit the .jks/.keystore file it points at either.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------- how to get these ---
#
# 1. Generate an upload keystore, once, outside this repository:
#
#      keytool -genkey -v -keystore prayer-walk-upload.jks -keyalg RSA \
#        -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 -alias prayer_walk_upload
#
#    `keytool` ships with any JDK (Android Studio bundles one). It will ask
#    for a store password, your name/organisation, and a key password — the
#    key password can be the same as the store password if you prefer one
#    secret to keep track of.
#
# 2. Move the resulting .jks into this `android/` directory (it's git-ignored
#    — see `.gitignore` — so it never gets committed) and also back it up
#    somewhere outside this repo, such as a password manager or an encrypted
#    backup. Losing it is recoverable via Play Console's key-loss process, but
#    only after a support round trip, so back it up.
#
# 3. Fill in the four values below. `storeFile` is resolved relative to this
#    `android/` directory — the bare filename below is correct as long as the
#    .jks sits directly in `android/`, which is where it normally lives. An
#    absolute path works too if you'd rather keep it elsewhere.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------- after first upload ---
#
# Play App Signing re-signs your upload with its own key before distributing
# it, so the certificate that matters to the outside world is Play's, not the
# one you just generated. After your FIRST successful upload to Play Console:
#
#   1. Play Console → your app → Release → Setup → App integrity →
#      App signing key certificate → copy the SHA-1 fingerprint.
#   2. Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → the Android
#      OAuth client for this app's package name → add that SHA-1.
#
# Skip this and Google Sign-In will keep working for your own installs (which
# use the upload key's SHA-1, already registered from local testing) and fail
# for everyone who installs from the Play Store — a classic "works for me"
# bug that only shows up in production.

storeFile=prayer-walk-upload.jks
storePassword=
keyAlias=prayer_walk_upload
keyPassword=
